FIELD 1a: Project name

HeatShield (repo name: heatshield-planner; UI brand: "HeatShield")

FIELD 1b: Short overview

HeatShield is a browser-based decision-support web app (hackathon prototype, demo-grade rigour, no login) that tells city authorities in India where to act first during a heatwave. It has four layers: a data layer that pulls live public data (weather forecast, OpenStreetMap sites and buildings, optional satellite imagery, user-uploaded official power-demand history), a risk engine that scores every ward 0-100 with a transparent weighted formula, a demand model that forecasts city- or state-level electricity demand from temperature, and an AI planning agent that calls these as tools, runs a deterministic cooling-centre optimiser, verifies its own output, and produces a ranked action plan with advisories in English plus Kannada or Hindi. Primary journey: pick a city → Run Planner → read the ranked plan, map and agent trace. The app is a pure frontend that calls public APIs directly. It never sends alerts to anyone.

FIELD 2: Problem statement

Pain: El Niño years bring severe Indian summers. Heat alerts are issued per city or district, but heat exposure varies sharply inside a city: dense, treeless wards run several °C hotter than leafy ones a few km away. At the same time, demand for fans and ACs spikes together, which causes outages that remove the cooling people depend on. Authorities decide where to open cooling centres and whom to warn first with no ward-level view, and mostly react after people are harmed.

Impact: The elderly, children and outdoor workers (delivery riders, construction workers, street vendors) bear the harm. Utilities cannot see the stress window coming. Cooling centres end up where they are convenient and not where risk is highest.

Outcome: Within one click, an officer sees (a) wards ranked by heat risk with the factors behind each score, (b) the best k cooling-centre sites and the percentage of high-risk wards they cover, (c) the forecast demand peak and the time window to advise load shifting, (d) a verified, source-cited plan that can be exported.

Non-goals:

No sending of SMS, email, push or any alert.
No ward-level electricity load (it is not publicly available).
No grid control or outage prediction per feeder.
No health records.
No native mobile app.

