FIELD 4: Core features

Must-have

F1 City selection: search a city by name through the geocoding service; result sets the city centre and bounding box. Default on first load: Bengaluru, resolved through the same search (not hardcoded coordinates).
F2 Ward loading: load ward polygons from the GeoJSON URL set in Settings. If no URL is set, generate 1 km grid cells over the city bounding box, flagged is_grid_cell = true, and label them "grid cells, not wards" everywhere.
F3 Forecast fetch: 7-day hourly forecast per ward sample point.
F4 Candidate sites: fetch cooling-centre candidates from OpenStreetMap.
F5 Building density and green cover: per ward, from OpenStreetMap.
F6 Satellite layer (optional at runtime): latest low-cloud Sentinel-2 NDVI and Landsat land-surface temperature per ward, with scene date and cloud cover. The app must run fully if this layer fails.
F7 Risk engine: weighted score 0-100 per ward, risk class, factor breakdown, live weight sliders.
F8 Demand model: upload official history, column mapping, quadratic regression on temperature, backtest, 7-day forecast with interval, stress window.
F9 Scenario toggle: live or stress_plus_2c, with persistent SIMULATED ribbon.
F10 Cooling-centre optimiser: deterministic greedy maximal coverage, with a with/without plan comparison.
F11 Planning agent: LLM function-calling loop over six allowlisted tools, capped at 12 steps.
F12 Verifier: checks the plan against tool outputs and forces up to 2 corrections.
F13 Template mode: if the LLM is unavailable, build the plan deterministically from the same tools.
F14 Action plan view and export: three prioritised cards, language switch, copy, and PDF download using the browser print-to-PDF flow.
F15 Agent trace: live-streamed step list with tool name, arguments, result summary, duration, status.
F16 Sources tab: per-source status, last fetched time, record count, retry, official link.
F17 Ward drawer and map layers: click a ward to see its breakdown and 7-day heat-index sparkline; layer toggles.
F18 Settings: ward GeoJSON URL, k, radius, weights, hazard thresholds, language.

Nice-to-have (only after all must-haves pass)

N1: Skippable 4-step first-run tour with no sample data.
N2: Dark/light theme toggle (dark is the default).
N3: Optional vulnerability upload (population or age groups) in F7.
N4: A small Python backend for satellite processing, only if browser raster reading proves unreliable.

Out of scope: alert dispatch, authentication, database, user accounts, feeder-level load, mobile native apps.

